            <ArticleTitle>A case report: Liraglutide as a novel treatment option in late dumping syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e6348</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/MD.0000000000006348</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="RATIONALE" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Postprandial hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia, known as late dumping syndrome, is a rare but often misdiagnosed complication after gastric surgery. The pathophysiological mechanisms are poorly understood and the treatment of this syndrome is challenging.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT CONCERNS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">New-onset postsurgical late dumping syndrome after Toupet fundoplication.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DIAGNOSES" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Sigstad Score, OGTT, CGM.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Daily subcutaneous injection of liraglutide (0.6 mg and 1.2 mg).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOMES" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Reduction in fasting and postprandial peak insulin level with improvement in symptomatic hypoglycemic events.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LESSONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Liraglutide may be a novel treatment option for postprandial hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia after gastric surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
